// Lesson 3.4 — Projectile Damage, Tuning & Playtest
// Closes the turret loop: something the turret fired has to hurt the player.
//
// Scaffolding: OUTLINE ONLY (level 3). The spec and the API are given and no
// code is. The student built the trigger-and-tag pickup in 2.1, the signed
// damage method in 2.2, the spawn-then-destroy lifecycle in 3.2, and the
// StatManager in 2.7 — so this lesson names the file, the callback, the
// decision and the cost, and stops there. The ask banner is the level marker:
// a level-3 lesson must not render a walkthrough, and verify-site refuses one
// that does.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the teacher's project), and the reason this page was
// rewritten:
//   Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile.prefab
//       tag E_Fireball, CircleCollider2D m_IsTrigger: 1 (radius 0.07 at a
//       localScale of 4), NO Rigidbody2D. It is moved by Projectile.cs with
//       MoveTowards on the transform, so it never needs one — the PLAYER's
//       Dynamic body is the body that makes the trigger fire.
//   Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs — the hit is handled on the RECEIVER:
//       void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
//       {
//           if (other.gameObject.CompareTag("E_Fireball"))
//           {
//               manager.ChangeHealth(-2);
//               Destroy(other.gameObject);
//           }
//           ...
//       }
//   Assets/Scripts/StatManager.cs — ChangeHealth(int) clamps above maxHealth
//   and calls Die() at or below zero. `manager` is the 2.7 reference found with
//   FindAnyObjectByType<StatManager>().
//
// THE OLD PAGE TAUGHT THE BULLET-SIDE MODEL: add a collider and a Rigidbody2D
// to the projectile, detect the hit on the bullet, look the player up with
// GetComponent<>(), and call a `TakeDamage()` method. None of that is the
// build. What the build teaches instead is the receiver-side rule — the object
// that owns the number is the object that reacts — and that the tag exists so
// the player can tell a fireball from a coin in one callback.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted, so the surviving socratic block
// keeps its three questions in order (reworded, never moved) and the decompose
// block keeps both of its turns (dc1:q2, dc1:q3) where they were.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// Level 3, so the renderer withholds what each part DOES and asks the student to
// say it — the parts and their doc pointers are the level-3 bargain. Every doc
// pointer is a lesson they have finished or a row of this lesson's own API
// table.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big: 'My bullets should actually hurt the player, and the fight should be hard without being unfair.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Give the bullet a name the player can recognise',
      does: 'The prefab ships tagged `E_Fireball`, with a trigger collider and no body. The tag is the whole identity — one word that separates this object from a coin, a potion and the turret that fired it.',
      doc: '2.1 — Coins: Triggers, Tags & Your First Stat (the tag-and-trigger recipe), and the `other.gameObject.CompareTag()` row of this lesson\'s API table',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Receive the hit on the object that owns the health',
      does: 'A branch in the player\'s own `OnTriggerEnter2D()` — the same callback that already handles the coin, the potion and the enemy. The thing that arrived does not do the damage; the thing it arrived at does.',
      doc: 'the `OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)` row of this lesson\'s API table, and your own 2.1 callback',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Spend health through the StatManager you built',
      does: '`manager.ChangeHealth(-2)` — the signed amount from 2.7, called on the reference the player already holds. The stat keeps its own rules: no health above `maxHealth`, and `Die()` at zero.',
      doc: '2.7 — Refactor Day: Moving Stats into the StatManager',
      href: '2.7.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Remove the bullet so one shot costs one hit',
      does: '`Destroy(other.gameObject)` inside the same branch — the cleanup 3.2 did on a lifetime, now triggered by a hit instead of a clock.',
      doc: '3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate(), Fire, Destroy(), and the `Destroy(Object obj)` row of this lesson\'s API table',
      href: '3.2.html',
    },
  ],
  why: 'Three of these four parts are lessons you have already finished, and the fourth is the one genuinely new idea: every script you wrote before today changed its own values. A fireball cannot reach the player\'s health, so the player reaches for the fireball instead — the object that owns the number is the object that reacts, and the bullet only has to be recognisable.',
  sizeTest: 'The split is finished when every part names something you could point at in the editor: a tag on a prefab, a branch in a callback, a method on a script, a call. If a part needs the word "and" twice to describe it, it is two parts.',
  turns: [
    'The player\'s callback does the damage; the fireball itself has no damage code at all. Say who owns the health number, and what the bullet would have to know about the player if it did the work itself.',
    'The `E_Fireball` branch sits two lines above the `Coin` branch, in the same callback. What would happen to the player if you merged those two into one `if`?',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>3.4 — Projectile Damage, Tuning &amp; Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret shoots, and every bullet passes clean through you. Close that loop — and notice
    that the bullet is not the thing that does the damage. Then set the numbers that decide whether
    the fight is tense or unfair.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make my bullet hurt the player?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('3.4', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code>, then
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> a <strong>Circle Collider 2D</strong> with
            <strong>Is Trigger</strong> ticked, <strong>Tag</strong> set to
            <code>E_Fireball</code>, and <em>no Rigidbody 2D</em>. Nothing here needs adding: a
            trigger needs a body on <em>one</em> of the two objects, and the player has had one since
            <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Transform → Scale</strong> is <code>4</code>, and the collider's
            radius is <code>0.07</code>: the green outline in the <strong>Scene view</strong> is
            about <code>0.28</code> world units across the art. A collider sized from the import
            bounds would not match what the player sees.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Edit → Project Settings → Tags and Layers → Tag</strong>: confirm
            <code>E_Fireball</code> exists and note the exact spelling and capitalisation. A tag
            that does not match is not an error, it is silence — the callback runs and does nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/Player</code>, then
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> the <strong>Box Collider 2D</strong>, the
            <strong>Rigidbody 2D</strong> set to <strong>Dynamic</strong>, and the
            <code>Player Controller</code> component. That body is what makes the fireball's trigger
            fire at all.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the code half is done — Game view → press Play</strong> and walk into a
            turret's line of fire: the Console line from <code>ChangeHealth()</code> drops by exactly
            your number, the fireball vanishes, and at zero your 2.7 <code>Die()</code> runs.
            Console silent? Check the tag spelling before the code, and the collider before the tag.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> a <strong>Turret</strong> instance, duplicate it
            (<strong>Ctrl+D</strong>) so the Overworld carries three. Give each a different
            <strong>Detection Radius</strong> — that collider is per-instance, so three copies of one
            prefab become three enemies with three reaches. The <em>timing</em> is not per-instance:
            <code>timeBetweenShots</code> is a field in <code>TurretController.cs</code>, so all three
            share it until you give it a serialized value.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it is</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code></td>
            <td>A callback Unity calls on the object that <em>received</em> the overlap — here, the player. You never call it.</td>
            <td>The hit report, on the frame the fireball's trigger touches the player's collider.</td>
            <td>One method has to serve every thing that can touch you — the fireball, the coin, the potion, the enemy. Each one is another branch in the same body.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>other.gameObject.CompareTag("E_Fireball")</code></td>
            <td>Identity check by tag, on the object that arrived.</td>
            <td>One check, and you know what hit you.</td>
            <td>Exact spelling, and the tag must exist. Ask it of <code>other.gameObject</code> — the thing that arrived — never of <code>gameObject</code>, which is you.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>manager.ChangeHealth(-2)</code></td>
            <td>A public call on the StatManager reference from 2.7.</td>
            <td>The signed amount that serves damage and healing, with the clamp and the death rule still owned by the stat.</td>
            <td>Everything public is a promise to every future caller, and the amount is a number written in the player's callback — not a knob anyone can tune without opening the script.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code></td>
            <td>Removes an object at the end of the frame.</td>
            <td>The cleanup 3.2 used on a lifetime, now on a hit.</td>
            <td>It has to name <code>other.gameObject</code>. <code>Destroy(gameObject)</code> would delete the player in the middle of a fireball, and nothing would warn you.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>E_Fireball</code></td>
            <td>A string in Project Settings → Tags and Layers, carried by the prefab.</td>
            <td>A word the player's callback can test, and the turret's own <code>Enemy</code> tag stays free for 4.6's melee hit.</td>
            <td>Stringly typed: the compiler cannot check a tag, so a typo compiles and ships.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for the hit.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The receiver:</strong> which object's script gets the callback, and why is it the player rather than the fireball? The fireball has a collider and no body — what would you have to add before a <code>Turret_Projectile</code> script could receive anything at all? (This is the model the project did <em>not</em> take; be able to say why.)</li>
            <li><strong>The branch:</strong> which tag does your condition test, and which object do you ask? Where does the new branch go relative to the coin, potion and enemy branches that are already in that method?</li>
            <li><strong>The damage:</strong> which method does the branch call, and which sign is the number you pass? Who decides what happens when health reaches zero — the player's branch, or the stat? Say where the clamp lives.</li>
            <li><strong>The cleanup:</strong> which object does the <code>Destroy()</code> call take, and what would <code>Destroy(gameObject)</code> do instead? Does the destroy come before the damage or after it?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
<li>The player's own callback does the damage, and the fireball carries no damage code at all. Which object owns the health number, and what would the bullet have to know about the player in order to do it itself?</li>
<li>The prefab ships with <em>Is Trigger</em> ticked. Describe what the player feels if you untick it and give the fireball a Dynamic body instead — and name the callback that would have to replace this one.</li>
<li>The fireball is tagged <code>E_Fireball</code> while the turret that fired it is <code>Enemy</code>. Why not reuse <code>Enemy</code>? Say what the player's callback would do in that case that it does not do now.</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <ul>
        <li>
            Swap seats and run a silent playtest, <a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> rules: you watch, they
            play, nobody speaks. Did they notice the turret before it fired, or was the first hit a
            surprise? Did they try to dodge? Then name the number you would move first and where it
            lives — the <strong>Detection Radius</strong> (Inspector, per turret),
            <code>timeBetweenShots</code> (script), <code>Projectile.speed</code> (script), or the
            damage amount (script, in the player's branch). Change one, play again, and say what the
            evidence was.
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain why the fireball needs no Rigidbody2D, and which object's body makes its trigger fire</li>
        <li>Explain why the damage happens on the player and not on the bullet, and what <code>other.gameObject</code> means in each line of the branch</li>
        <li>Say which sign you pass to <code>ChangeHealth()</code>, and who clamps at <code>maxHealth</code> and calls <code>Die()</code></li>
        <li>Name the four numbers that decide difficulty, the file or Inspector each lives in, and your value for each</li>
        <li>Run a silent playtest and make one evidence-based change, then commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — the complete turret system</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the hit detection reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — trigger callbacks, tags, and <code>Destroy()</code></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — find this lesson by the question you were asking</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.1.html">4.1 — The Enemy: Patrol</a>, where Unit 4 begins and something starts hunting you.</p>
</div>`;
